WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.164 --> 00:00:04.413
We present DexEyeGrasp, a framework for dexterous grasping in clutter using cameras

2
00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:05.744
mounted on the fingertips.

3
00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:10.621
Introduction.

4
00:00:11.576 --> 00:00:15.973
The overview camera initially sees the target, but the approaching hand occludes

5
00:00:15.973 --> 00:00:16.137
it.

6
00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:18.776
Fingertip views complement this fixed viewpoint.

7
00:00:20.081 --> 00:00:21.906
We begin with a global view.

8
00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:25.752
Color-coded FingerEye cameras provide dexterous viewpoints.

9
00:00:25.752 --> 00:00:29.988
We identify the target, then search through clutter and grasp it.

10
00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:31.871
Method.

11
00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:34.276
Four training components.

12
00:00:35.876 --> 00:00:39.399
First, reinforcement learning trains a privileged state-based teacher.

13
00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:44.863
The visual student learns teacher actions from FingerEye images and proprioception.

14
00:00:46.243 --> 00:00:50.367
Self-supervised heads train jointly with the visual policy to predict current and

15
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.436
next target geometry.

16
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:56.832
Real-image adaptation uses the policy and geometry heads together, training a residual

17
00:00:56.832 --> 00:00:58.996
encoder while freezing the original network.

18
00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:01.477
Learning in confined clutter.

19
00:01:02.503 --> 00:01:04.857
Direct learning in clutter is difficult.

20
00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:08.917
Target-grid curricula divide the task among nine specialist teachers.

21
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:12.978
We distill their skills into a unified teacher, then train with fully

22
00:01:12.978 --> 00:01:14.096
randomized clutter.

23
00:01:14.096 --> 00:01:18.216
Physical randomization improves robustness to objects and controllers.

24
00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:21.111
Improving visuomotor learning.

25
00:01:22.101 --> 00:01:25.248
The current head estimates the camera-to-target vector.

26
00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:29.254
The next head combines FingerEye RGB with the action to predict future

27
00:01:29.254 --> 00:01:29.769
geometry.

28
00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:32.446
Transferring to the real world.

29
00:01:33.523 --> 00:01:38.749
Visual randomization varies backgrounds, lighting, and image quality during student training.

30
00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:42.963
These diverse observations prepare the visual policy for real-world scenes.

31
00:01:44.363 --> 00:01:48.021
About fifty teleoperated trajectories provide geometric labels.

32
00:01:48.021 --> 00:01:54.640
Adaptation corrects real-image features through geometry, without requiring successful demonstrations or imitating

33
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.394
the operator.

34
00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:57.351
Experiments.

35
00:01:58.449 --> 00:02:02.302
We evaluate fifteen and thirty objects, both on a table and inside

36
00:02:02.302 --> 00:02:02.652
a box.

37
00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.552
Success requires lifting the target.

38
00:02:06.552 --> 00:02:09.296
SAM two tracks it from an initial manual box.

39
00:02:10.526 --> 00:02:14.013
Across ten trials per setting, success reaches nine out of ten with

40
00:02:14.013 --> 00:02:17.239
fifteen objects, and eight out of ten with thirty, both on the

41
00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:18.332
table and in the box.

42
00:02:19.844 --> 00:02:23.021
DexEyeGrasp grasps different target objects in clutter.

43
00:02:34.195 --> 00:02:36.296
We also test forty and fifty objects.

44
00:02:38.119 --> 00:02:43.305
These denser demonstrations show fingertip feedback during grasping when surrounding objects restrict

45
00:02:43.305 --> 00:02:44.434
visibility and access.

46
00:02:51.732 --> 00:02:57.066
DexEyeGrasp combines dexterous FingerEye viewpoints, curriculum learning and self-supervision for real-world grasping.

47
00:02:57.066 --> 00:02:59.462
More information is available on our project website.
